<Layout title="Weighted selection of a simulated design"><Header /><article><a href="/#notes">← Technical Notes</a><h1>Weighted selection of a simulated design</h1><h2>Why rank simulated designs?</h2><p>The reaction cube project explores how flywheel geometry influences nonlinear motion. A candidate that moves quickly may also require more mass or a higher rotational speed. Ranking makes these trade-offs explicit instead of selecting a design from a single attractive result.</p><h2>Compare successful runs</h2><p>The study evaluated 19,088 simulated cases across 157 flywheel geometries. The optimisation workflow compares successful runs using weighted min–max normalisation. Each criterion is scaled relative to the tested candidates so quantities with different units can contribute to a combined score.</p><h2>Weights express priorities</h2><p>The flywheel routine balances component mass, tipping time and target RPM. Changing their weights changes the preferred trade-off. The ranking therefore represents a design decision under chosen priorities, rather than a universal measure of quality.</p><h2>Refine and specify</h2><p>The search refines candidate parameters around the best tested design. The main script passes the selected flywheel geometry into a servo-specification search constrained by a power limit. This connects simulated behaviour to the process of defining hardware requirements.</p><h2>Interpret the result</h2><p>The result is the best tested candidate under the model assumptions, search bounds and selected weights. It is not proof of a global optimum or a physical balancing demonstration. Hardware validation and sensitivity to modelling assumptions remain important next steps.</p></article><Footer /></Layout>
